== Overview and historical background <sec:varieties-overview>

All groups considered in this chapter will be finite. Recall from the last
chapter (Section~@sec:finite-generation) that if $R$ is a Noetherian ring then
$H^* (G, R)$ is finitely generated as a ring over $R$. It is also graded
commutative#idx("graded", "commutative")#idx("commutative", "graded")
(Section~3.2 of Volume~I) in the sense that
$ x y = (-1)^(deg(x) deg(y)) y x. $
In particular, if $R = k$ is a field of characteristic $p$, then
+ for $p = 2$, $H^* (G, k)$ is commutative;
+ for $p$ odd, the subring $H^"ev" (G, k)$ generated by elements of even degree
  is commutative, while elements of odd degree square to zero.

Thus we define#idx("$H^dot (G, k)$")
$
  H^dot (G, k) = cases(
    H^* (G, k) & "if" p = 2,
    H^"ev" (G, k) & "if" p "is odd"
  )
$
so that $H^dot (G, k)$ is a finitely generated commutative graded ring over $k$.
For any finitely generated commutative ring over $k$, the maximal ideals form
the points of an affine algebraic variety. We denote by $V_G$#idx("$V_G$") the
variety corresponding to $H^dot (G, k)$. Quillen [@bib:Quillen1971b,
@bib:Quillen1971c] (1971) investigated the variety $V_G$ and showed that it is
stratified by pieces coming from the elementary abelian subgroups#idx(
  "elementary",
  "abelian group",
)#idx("group", "elementary abelian") of $G$. In particular, he showed that the
dimension of $V_G$ (i.e., the Krull dimension of $H^dot (G, k)$) is equal to the
maximal rank $r_p (G)$ of an elementary abelian $p$-subgroup of $G$, as was
conjectured by Atiyah (unpublished) and independently by Swan [@bib:Swan1971].

Five years later, Chouinard [@bib:Chouinard1976] (1976) proved that a
$k G$-module $M$ is projective if and only if the restriction $M down_E$ is
projective for every elementary abelian subgroup $E$ of $G$.

Another five years passed before Alperin and Evens [@bib:Alperin1981] (1981)
found a common generalisation of these theorems. They defined the
_complexity_#idx("complexity") $c_G (M)$ of a finitely generated $k G$-module
$M$ to be the rate of growth of a minimal resolution of $M$. More precisely, if
$ dots.c -> P_2 -> P_1 -> P_0 -> M -> 0 $
is the minimal resolution, then $c_G (M)$ is defined to be the least integer $s$
such that there is a constant $kappa > 0$ with
$ dim_k P_n lt.eq kappa . n^(s-1) quad "for" quad n > 0. $
#source(166)Thus for example a module $M$ has complexity zero if and only if its
minimal resolution stops at some finite stage. Since projective modules are also
injective (Proposition~3.1.2 of Volume~I), this means that $M$ is projective.
Similarly, $M$ has complexity one if and only if the modules in the minimal
resolution have bounded dimension. We shall see in Section~@sec:periodic-modules
that this happens if and only if the minimal resolution repeats with finite
period (we then say that $M$ is _periodic_#idx("periodic module")#idx(
  "module",
  "periodic",
)).

Alperin and Evens proved that the complexity of a module $M$ is equal to the
maximal complexity of $M down_E$ as $E$ ranges over the elementary abelian
$p$-subgroups of $G$. The case of complexity zero is Chouinard's theorem, while
the case $M = k$, the trivial module, is Quillen's theorem on the dimension of
$V_G$, after a little re-interpretation.

Meanwhile, Carlson [@bib:Carlson1981a, @bib:Carlson1983] had been investigating
certain varieties associated to a module $M$. The _cohomological variety_
$V_G (M)$#idx("cohomological variety")#idx("$V_G (M)$") is defined to be the
subvariety of $V_G$ defined by the ideal in
$H^dot (G, k) = Ext^dot_(k G) (k, k)$ of elements annihilating
$Ext^*_(k G) (M, M)$. The _rank variety_#idx("rank", "variety")#idx(
  "variety",
  "rank",
) $V^sharp_E (M)$ is only defined for an elementary abelian group $E$, and its
definition does not involve cohomology. Carlson showed that $V_E (M)$ and
$V^sharp_E (M)$ both have dimension equal to the complexity of $M$. He
conjectured that these varieties were equal, and showed how this would imply
that $V_G (M)$ is well behaved for tensor products and restrictions of modules.
Avrunin and Scott [@bib:Avrunin1982] proved Carlson's conjecture#idx(
  "Carlson's",
  "conjecture",
)#idx("conjecture", "Carlson's"), and deduced that $V_G (M)$ is stratified by
pieces coming from elementary abelian subgroups of $G$, in analogy with
Quillen's theorem.

Our presentation will differ considerably from this historical order, and
benefits from later simplifications by Carlson, Evens and others. The following
portmanteau theorem gives some of the main properties of the varieties
$V_G (M)$.

#theorem[
  + If $M$ is a finitely generated $k G$-module, then the dimension of $V_G (M)$
    is equal to the complexity $c_G (M)$. In particular $V_G (M) = {0}$ if and
    only if $M$ is projective.
  + $V_G (M_1 plus.o M_2) = V_G (M_1) union V_G (M_2)$.
  + $V_G (M_1 times.o M_2) = V_G (M_1) inter V_G (M_2)$.
  + $V_G (M) = V_G (M^*) = V_G (Omega(M)) = V_G (Omega^(-1)(M))$.
  + Denote by $res^*_(G, H) : V_H -> V_G$ the map induced by
    $res_(G, H) : H^dot (G, k) -> H^dot (H, k)$. Then
    $V_H (M) = (res^*_(G, H))^(-1) V_G (M)$.
  + $V_G (M) = display(union.big_(E lt.eq G \ "elemab")) res^*_(G, E) V_E (M)$.
  + If $0 != zeta in Ext^n_(k G) (k, k)$ is represented by a map
    $hat(zeta) : Omega^n k -> k$ ($n$ even if $p$ is odd) with kernel $L_zeta$
    then $V_G (L_zeta) = V_G chevron.l zeta chevron.r$ is the hypersurface in
    $V_G$ determined by $zeta$.
  + If $V_G (M) = V_1 union V_2$ with $V_1 inter V_2 = {0}$ then
    $M tilde.equiv M_1 plus.o M_2$ with $V_G (M_1) = V_1$ and $V_G (M_2) = V_2$.
] <th:variety-properties>

The proofs may be found in Sections~@sec:module-varieties, @sec:modules-l-zeta
and @sec:andrews.
